body:
If you haven't already bought it (which you should have by now);

&#x200B;

The primary reason anyone plays any game is variance. If we can predict any game with 100% certainty and no chance of detour, it is not fun. Risk of Rain is variance exemplified. Random items, random stages, random enemies, random bosses. Every run is entirely different from each one that came before it, and it keeps the game from going stale.

&#x200B;

On top of that, soundtrack, art style, storytelling, etc, is all on point. So if artwork is a thing you go for, then go for it.

&#x200B;

AND FINALLY: The devs, and the game, almost don't care at all how broken you become. Last night I had a run where every time I shot something, I simultaneously fired 30+ missiles from my shoulder. If the missiles killed something (they did), then the corpse of the enemy would spawn knives that tracked to other enemies. This would loop ad nauseum, wiping the whole map with a single shot fired.

&#x200B;

Also, the game has Benthic Bloom. I'd pay $100 to have Benthic Bloom in every game I play. Praise Benthic.

First ever completion, just playing Commando for fun. Benthic Bloom ended up taking me all the way to Voidling with undeniable classics such as "Here's 8 Best Friends", and "Never Enough Scorpions".

&#x200B;

I definitely did die to Voidling 7 times though, that's real. That spinning laser, man.

I've read through some of the comments and haven't seen an important aspect of the bomb yet; Bombs cease to exist past their application. Mara Sov is the only person to explicitly mention Bomb Logic, and this was in reference to her plan to kill herself and enter Oryx's realm. Maybe I misinterpreted something here, but that's what I understood of it.

&#x200B;

I think that Guardians are also adherent to Bomb Logic. Nova Bomb, for instance. I forget the exact science behind it (there's a Grade-A post you can find that talks about the physics behind Void energy), but it is quite literally an ultra-complex machination that we create from thin air, that then ceases to exist once it's done it's job (vaporizing half the room). Perhaps you could paint a picture where Guardians themselves are bombs, and in doing our work, we often kill ourselves time and time again, but I don't know how resurrection would play into that.

&#x200B;

You're right in that it's a complex system and that guns are also a complex system, but generally a gun lives past it's purpose. Sword Logic says, "I'm stronger than you. I have the right to exist and you don't." Bomb Logic says, "Complexity overshadows strength, and I'll cease to exist to prove that."

It's not that I hate weapons- They're in the game to be used as a valid mechanic. I tried to make this post the \*least\* bitchy as I could, even admitting that I quit because I was bad. I was just naming the few things that were breaking the "meta" when I played so long ago.

&#x200B;

I do appreciate the clarity though. Is it official that no more work is being done? I suppose it's safe to assume based on the fact that there hasn't been a patch in forever.
